You selected count, which can create three EC2 instances. However, the instance names dev, test, and prod are distinct, meaningful keys, making for_each a better fit.
variable "environments" {
  default = ["dev", "test", "prod"]
}

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  count         = length(var.environments)
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = var.environments[count.index]
  }
}

Resource addresses are index-based:
- aws_instance.app[0] → dev
- aws_instance.app[1] → test
- aws_instance.app[2] → prod
If you remove test from the middle, the indices shift. Terraform may propose updating or replacing resources associated with the shifted indices, depending on the changes. This can be risky.

Option 2: for_each — recommended here
variable "environments" {
  default = toset(["dev", "test", "prod"])
}

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  for_each      = var.environments
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = each.key
  }
}

Addresses use stable keys:
- aws_instance.app["dev"]
- aws_instance.app["test"]
- aws_instance.app["prod"]
Removing test means Terraform can target that specific instance for destruction without shifting the other environments' addresses.
Remember this interview rule
- count — when you need a number of similar instances and index-based identity is acceptable.
- for_each — when resources have meaningful identities, such as environment names, usernames, or subnet keys.

